.TH AR 1 "March 15, 1972" "UNIX Fourth Edition" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v4-ar \- archive and library maintainer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ar
.I key afile name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I ar
maintains groups of files combined into a single archive file.
Its main use is to create and update library files as used by the loader.
It can also be used for similar purposes.
.PP
.I key
is one character from the set \fBdrtux\fR, optionally concatenated with
\fBv\fR.
.I afile
is the archive file.
The
.I names
are constituent files in the archive file.
The meanings of the
.I key
characters are:
.TP
.B d
Delete the named files from the archive file.
.TP
.B r
Replace the named files in the archive file.
If the archive file does not exist, create it.
If the named files are not in the archive file, append them.
.TP
.B t
Print a table of contents of the archive file.
If no names are given, list all files in the archive.
If names are given, list only those files.
.TP
.B u
Similar to \fBr\fR, except that only files that have been modified are replaced.
If no names are given, replace all files in the archive that have been modified.
.TP
.B x
Extract the named files.
If no names are given, extract all files in the archive.
In neither case does \fBx\fR alter the archive file.
.TP
.B v
Verbose mode.
.I ar
gives a file-by-file description of how a new archive file is made from the
old archive and the constituent files.
The following abbreviations are used:
.RS
.TP
.B c
copy
.TP
.B a
append
.TP
.B d
delete
.TP
.B r
replace
.TP
.B x
extract
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/vtm?
temporary file
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBv4-ld\fR(1), \fBv4-archive\fR(5)
.SH BUGS
The \fBtv\fR option should be implemented as a table with more information.
.PP
There should be a way to specify the placement of a new file in an archive.
Currently, it is placed at the end.
.PP
Since
.I ar
has not been rewritten to deal properly with the new file-system modes,
extracted files have mode 666.
